 {"id":"CF-062","title":"P5 — lane_target.py scan --allow-remote cannot fail: a registry where no lane was actually verified still exits 0 with ok:true","status":"pending","priority":"P5","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory-readme-qa lane at tip 9880018. The probe below builds a two-row registry fixture (one `builder@node-01:/srv/cf` row, one local row) in a temp file. `scan <fixture>` without the flag exits 4 (bad:1), as designed; `scan <fixture> --allow-remote` prints {\"ok\":true,\"bad\":0} and exits 0 with the remote row carrying only `verified: remote-structural-only` - the same aggregate verdict and exit code as a registry where every lane really was verified (scripts/lane_target.py:296-306 folds both classes into one boolean). specs/NODE.md:87 says a remote target 'is never silently treated as verified'; the per-row field keeps the distinction but the aggregate, which is what a gate or CI job reads, erases it, so the documented way to include remote lanes produces a green check that cannot fail.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; prints {\"ok\":true,\"bad\":0} and exits 0 today): p=$(mktemp) && printf '%s\\n' \"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"remote-lane\\\",\\\"repo_url\\\":\\\"local:$PWD\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"builder@node-01:/srv/cf\\\"}\" \"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"local-lane\\\",\\\"repo_url\\\":\\\"local:$PWD\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"$PWD\\\"}\" > \"$p\" && python3 -B scripts/lane_target.py scan \"$p\" --allow-remote; echo \"rc=$?\"\n1. `scan --allow-remote` output carries top-level counts by verification class (`verified_local`, `structural_only`, `failed`) and its exit code distinguishes them: 0 only when every row is verified local, 5 when at least one row is structural-only, 4 when any row failed.\n2. A fixture proves each class: all-local -> 0; mixed with --allow-remote -> 5 and a printed `WARN structural-only: 1 lane not verified`; a remote row without the flag -> 4 as today.\n3. The same fixture run twice produces identical output (the check is deterministic).\n4. specs/NODE.md's scan bullet states the exit-code contract and tests/test_lane_target.py covers it; `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS.","created_at":"2026-11-03T12:48:52Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T12:51:57Z","probe":"p=$(mktemp) && printf '%s\\n' \"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"remote-lane\\\",\\\"repo_url\\\":\\\"local:$PWD\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"builder@node-01:/srv/cf\\\"}\" \"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"local-lane\\\",\\\"repo_url\\\":\\\"local:$PWD\\\",\\\"workdir\\\":\\\"$PWD\\\"}\" > \"$p\" && python3 -B scripts/lane_target.py scan \"$p\" --allow-remote; echo \"rc=$?\""}
 {"id":"CF-063","title":"verify-node.sh cannot show its usage before BUNKER_SERVER is set and rejects --help","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 at tree 9880018 from a git archive export with an isolated HOME. verify-node.sh expands ${BUNKER_SERVER:?} at line 17 before parsing arguments, so a caller with no server configured cannot see how to call it: --help exits 1 with the line-17 abort. With the variable set, --help exits 2 as unknown arg, and only a bare invocation prints the usage line (exit 2). bunker-server-install.sh has the same placement at line 14, but README supplies that export three lines above the command; verify-node.sh has no README coverage. Found while auditing CF-041; the measurements are in docs/audits/code-factory-CF-041.md.","acceptance_criteria":"1. scripts/verify-node.sh --help prints the usage line without requiring BUNKER_SERVER, exiting 2, and a bare invocation does the same. 2. With BUNKER_SERVER set, --help prints the usage line instead of unknown arg. 3. The missing-variable abort still names BUNKER_SERVER for commands that need it. 4. tests/selftest.sh gains a check that proves the new help behaviour can FAIL, and prints SELFTEST: PASS.","created_at":"2026-11-03T12:54:19Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T12:54:19Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"413e19e2c50c7c7dd96329d3096b3d66d4cdbc428d1b085e3acaf88964b36d1f"}}
