 {"id":"CF-066","title":"P3 — project-node.sh discards the harness adapter's exit status: an installer that dies without printing a FAIL row still yields NODE: PASS and a written node manifest","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory QA lane at 3735c99 (CF-055's fix bb99a5c is an ancestor; `bash tests/selftest.sh` = SELFTEST: PASS at that tip). scripts/project-node.sh:128 captures `rc=$?` from the harness-adapter invocation and never reads it again; the only thing that reaches the parent counter is line 129's `grep -c '^  FAIL'` on the adapter log. An adapter that dies before printing any row (crashes, `set -u` abort, missing interpreter, a lib.sh `say` then a hard shell error) therefore contributes 0 to FAILED, the [90] manifest gate at :176 sees FAILED=0 and WRITES .node/node.json, and :197 prints NODE: PASS and exits 0. Fixture proof in the probe field: a copy of the script tree with an adapter that prints `install-dead.sh: line 9: SOME_UNSET_VAR: unbound variable` on stderr and exits 1 yields `rc=0 summary=NODE: PASS printed_fail_rows=0 manifest=YES`; the control with an adapter that calls lib.sh bad() yields `rc=1 summary=NODE: FAIL (1) ... manifest=no`. So the parent can only see failures the child chose to print, which is exactly the defect class AGENTS.md rule 2 forbids ('a check that prints FAIL but cannot reach the caller' — here the check never prints and still cannot reach the caller). CF-055 fixed the count for printed rows only, and its AC3 ('exit codes are unchanged for both cases') is satisfied while this half of the blind spot stays open: a node is admitted with a dead installer, and the admitted state is the one the manifest records. Not reachable from this repo's own selftest because every fixture adapter either prints a FAIL row or exits 0.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; prints `rc=0 summary=NODE: PASS printed_fail_rows=0 manifest=YES` today).\n1. tests/selftest.sh gains a fixture adapter that exits nonzero WITHOUT printing a `  FAIL` row: the run prints a parent-side row naming the adapter and its exit status (e.g. `FAIL  harness:dead — installer exited 1`), summarises `NODE: FAIL (n)` with n>=1, exits 1, and writes no .node/node.json.\n2. The printed-row path still counts exactly once: an adapter that prints k FAIL rows and exits 1 still summarises NODE: FAIL (k) (assert the integer against `grep -c '^  FAIL'`, so a new exit-status row is not double-counted with the rows it accompanies).\n3. No false negative: an adapter that exits 0 and prints no FAIL row still yields NODE: PASS, rc=0, manifest written — both directions in the same run.\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip, with a pre-fix control (current project-node.sh) showing the PASS-on-dead-adapter result the fixture now rejects.\n5. AGENTS.md rule 2's 'every check must be able to fail' is named in the commit message for this row, since the fixture is a gate-integrity change rather than a feature.","created_at":"2026-11-03T14:13:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T14:13:00Z","probe":"p=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p $p/s/harness $p/bin; cp scripts/project-node.sh scripts/lib.sh scripts/disk_floor.py $p/s/; printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\\nprintf 200\\n' > $p/bin/curl; chmod +x $p/bin/curl; printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n. \"$(dirname \"$0\")/../lib.sh\"\\nsay \"harness: dead adapter\"\\nexit 1\\n' > $p/s/harness/install-dead.sh; chmod +x $p/s/harness/install-dead.sh; git init -q -b main $p/w; git -C $p/w -c user.email=q@q -c user.name=q commit -q --allow-empty -m f; git clone -q --bare $p/w $p/o.git; h=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p $h/.node; printf '{\"agent\":\"f\"}' > $h/.node/crier.json; chmod 600 $h/.node/crier.json; env -i HOME=$h PATH=$p/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin NODE_DISK_FLOOR=1 NODE_PROJECT=p NODE_HARNESS=dead CRIER_URL=http://f.invalid:8767 bash $p/s/project-node.sh --apply --project p --repo file://$p/o.git --ref main > $p/out.log 2>&1; rc=$?; echo \"rc=$rc summary=$(grep -E '^NODE: ' $p/out.log | tail -1) printed_fail_rows=$(grep -c '^  FAIL' $p/out.log) manifest=$([ -f $h/.node/node.json ] && echo YES || echo no)\"; rm -rf $p $h"}
 {"id":"CF-067","title":"P3 — the three newest closures (CF-041, CF-054, CF-055) carry evidence digests that resolve to no git object and, for CF-055, a verdict that exists nowhere: closure evidence is shape-valid and content-unresolvable","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory QA lane at 3735c99. (1) CF-041 detail.evidence[last].tree_fp=1caff4972c22fb6c69e4fa34429dcfb6237bb7a5da0a99a972f80d7823f655f8 -> `git cat-file -t` answers `fatal: Not a valid object name`; the row's own commit_hash fbf1f3a has tree fed102d5a4e9. (2) CF-054 tree_fp=f3825569c9c9... also unresolvable, while its commit d088bc6 has tree c88366f5470a; its run_id d851a4c1 IS a real committed verdict dir that binds d088bc6 and passes, so only the digest is false. (3) CF-055 stores run_id `bb99a5c` (a commit abbreviation, not a run), artifacts [scripts/project-node.sh, tests/selftest.sh] (the implementation files, not evidence), and worker_summary cites verdict 20b17c27 — `find .gitreins -maxdepth 4 -type d -name '20b17c27*'` returns 0 matches, so the Tier-2 verdict the closure leans on is not in the repository. Its tree_fp da0288bfb44e DOES equal bb99a5c's tree, so the field is applied inconsistently rather than never implemented. Consequence: a QA or releng tick re-checking these rows cannot bind the recorded digest to a tree or the recorded verdict to a file; CF-059 filed this class for CF-036 (still pending) and CF-045/046/047 cover the same family, so the defect reproduces on rows filed AFTER those were written. Nothing here re-opens CF-041/CF-054/CF-055's acceptance: their ACs reproduce (see the tick's evidence file); what fails is the evidence pointer a future reader is told to trust.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; prints two `fatal: Not a valid object name` lines and `CF-055 verdict 20b17c27 dirs: 0` today).\n1. `scripts/boardctl validate` (or the selftest board arm, whichever owns the check) FAILs a fixture row whose `detail.evidence[].tree_fp` is not resolvable as a tree by `git cat-file -t` and whose `run_id` resolves neither to a `.gitreins/history/*/<run_id>/` directory nor to a commit in history; it PASSES a fixture row whose tree_fp equals `git rev-parse <commit_hash>^{tree}` and whose run_id names a committed verdict directory — both directions in one run, and the FAIL names the row id and the offending field.\n2. CF-041, CF-054 and CF-055 each gain an APPENDED (never rewritten) evidence entry whose tree_fp resolves and whose run_id names a committed verdict path — or the field is replaced by `none:<reason>` per docs/traceability-doctrine.md (CF-047 defines the fields; the doc and the rows must agree after the fix, in the same commit).\n3. The regression is fixed at the writer, not only in the rows: the tool that stamped these entries refuses an unresolvable digest, proven by a fixture that attempts to write one and gets a nonzero exit naming the reason.\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip.","created_at":"2026-11-03T14:13:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T14:21:21Z","probe":"for r in CF-041 CF-054; do jq -r --arg r \"$r\" 'select(.id==$r)|.detail.evidence|last|\"\\($r) run_id=\\(.run_id) tree_fp=\\(.tree_fp)\"' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl; done; for f in $(jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-041\" or .id==\"CF-054\")|.detail.evidence|last|.tree_fp' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl); do echo \"tree_fp ${f:0:12} -> $(git cat-file -t \"$f\" 2>&1)\"; done; echo \"CF-055 verdict 20b17c27 dirs: $(find .gitreins -maxdepth 4 -type d -name '20b17c27*' | wc -l)\"","foreman_note":"REVIEW 2026-11-03 (tick code-factory-review-2026-11-03-14-18-13): re-measured this row's sub-claims at origin/main e46b870 from a throwaway clone (not the candidate checkout). (a) TITLE OVER-CLAIMS, body is right: only 2 of the 3 digests fail to resolve. CF-055's tree_fp da0288bfb44ef6411281ed6b6437df0ea94870a5 answers `git cat-file -t` = tree and equals `git rev-parse bb99a5c^{tree}`; CF-041's FIRST evidence entry 4b97e25e4e41b51a07c5bd703a4887f102ffbbf5 also resolves as a tree. Unresolvable are CF-041 detail.evidence[last] 1caff497... and CF-054 f3825569..., both 64-hex and both `fatal: Not a valid object name`. The body already says the field is 'applied inconsistently rather than never implemented'; the title asserts all three and will send a fixer at a digest that is already correct. (b) AC-1 CANNOT FAIL THE ROW IT NAMES: its run_id clause accepts 'or a commit in history', and CF-055's run_id IS a commit abbreviation (bb99a5c), so a writer stamping commit abbreviations as run_id passes the new gate while the missing verdict stays missing. Restrict run_id to a committed .gitreins/history/*/<run_id>/ directory and leave commit binding to commit_hash. (c) SUB-CLAIM 3 CONFIRMED independently: .gitreins/history/2026-11-03 holds 17 verdict dirs, none with task_id CF-055; `grep -rl '\"bb99a5c' .gitreins/history` = 0 hits, so the Tier-2 PASS the CF-055 closure cites (verdict 20b17c27) has no committed artifact. (d) Corroboration, NOT a new row: CF-066's probe reproduces exactly at e46b870 — dead adapter yields rc=0 summary=NODE: PASS printed_fail_rows=0 manifest=YES; control yields rc=1 summary=NODE: FAIL (1) manifest=no."}
 {"id":"CF-068","title":"P3 — project-node.sh proves bus identity by FILE EXISTENCE: a 0-byte mode-600 crier.json passes crier_identity, so a node with no bus identity reports PASS on the layer that exists to catch exactly that","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory releng-QA lane at e46b870 (fresh clone of the published tip, clean porcelain). scripts/project-node.sh:137 is `if [ -f \"$STATE/crier.json\" ]`; on that test alone it prints `ok \"crier_identity\" \"$STATE/crier.json\"` (:138), then :139 checks the file mode (600) and nothing else. No JSON parse, no required-key check, no size check: the layer named `crier_identity` in its own failure text (\"a node without an identity is invisible\") proves only that a path exists. The probe field is the measurement: a 0-byte ~/.node/crier.json with mode 600 prints `PASS  crier_identity` and `PASS  crier_mode — 600`, while the same tree with the file absent prints `FAIL  crier_identity — absent — stage a bundle and re-run` (one more FAIL, 4 vs 3). So an empty, truncated, or fabricated bundle is indistinguishable from a real one on this layer, and layer 90 admits the node on that basis (the manifest at :183 records only bus.base, never an agent id, so nothing downstream re-checks it either). docs/OPERATIONS.md:40 states the secure bundle workflow is a requirement, not a supplied operation, and warns \"Do not hand-create a fake identity\" — the current check certifies precisely that fake. docs/THREAT-MODEL.md:44 already records that only the mode is checked. Distinct from CF-051/CF-056 (node-create.sh stages) and from the ACCEPTANCE-PLAN's CF-111 manifest-schema gap: this is the layer-30 admission check itself certifying content it never opens.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root; prints `PASS  crier_identity` and `PASS  crier_mode` for a 0-byte bundle today, and `FAIL  crier_identity — absent` for the control).\n1. `./scripts/project-node.sh --check` on a 0-byte or unparseable `$HOME/.node/crier.json` FAILs `crier_identity` with a reason that names the defect (empty / not JSON), and the FAIL is counted in `NODE: FAIL (n)`. A valid bundle fixture still PASSes both `crier_identity` and `crier_mode`.\n2. The required content is the one specs/NODE.md already names for bus identity (agent id and the bus base at minimum) and the spec text and the check ship in the same commit — no new undocumented keys.\n3. A mode-644 valid bundle still FAILs `crier_mode` (the mode test must survive the content test), both directions asserted in the same run.\n4. tests/selftest.sh gains a fixture for this: the pre-fix control (current script, 0-byte bundle) shows `PASS  crier_identity`, the post-fix run shows `FAIL`, and `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip.\n5. AGENTS.md rule 2 (every check must be able to fail) is named in the commit message, because this is a gate-integrity change, not a new feature.","created_at":"2026-11-03T14:23:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T14:23:00Z","probe":"h=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$h/.node\"; : > \"$h/.node/crier.json\"; chmod 600 \"$h/.node/crier.json\"; echo \"--- empty bundle ---\"; HOME=\"$h\" CRIER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9 ./scripts/project-node.sh --check --project demo 2>&1 | grep -E 'crier_identity|crier_mode'; echo \"--- absent bundle ---\"; h2=$(mktemp -d); HOME=\"$h2\" CRIER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9 ./scripts/project-node.sh --check --project demo 2>&1 | grep -E 'crier_identity|crier_mode'; rm -rf \"$h\" \"$h2\""}
